<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="fo1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>FO1: Solutions to Differential Equations</title>

    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can determine if a function or family of functions is a solution to a given differential equation, classify a differential equation as linear or nonlinear, and identify its order.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <subsection xml:id="ode-what-is">
        <title>What Is a Differential Equation?</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-ode-pde">
            <title>(Ordinary/Partial) Differential Equation</title>

            <idx><h>differential equation (ordinary)</h></idx>
            <idx><h>differential equation (partial)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>differential equation</term> is an equation which
                    relates a function to its derivatives.
                    There are two kinds of differential equations: ordinary
                    differential equations (ODEs) and partial differential
                    equations (PDEs).
                    In this course, we will restrict our focus to ordinary
                    differential equations.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An example of an ordinary differential equation is the
                    familiar
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                        \dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x
                        </mrow>
                    </md>
                    which asks the question: "What function y(x) has a
                    derivative equal to 2x?" The solution to this differential
                    equation is the family of functions
                    <md>
                        y(x) = x^2 + C
                    </md>
                    where C is an arbitrary constant.
                    We can check that this is a solution by differentiating;
                    <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^2 + C) = 2x</m>, so we have have
                    satisfied the condition stated by the differential equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Other examples of ordinary differential equations are
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{dy}{dx} = ky
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        \sin(t) + x''(t) + x'(t) = t
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        xe^x + \lrpar{\dfrac{dy}{dx}}^2 = 0
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Below are examples of partial differential equations:
                </p>

                <p>
                    The wave equation:
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{\partial^2f}{\partial x^2} = \dfrac{\partial^2f}{\partial t^2}
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    The heat equation:
                    <md>
                        c^2 \dfrac{\partial^2f}{\partial x^2} = \dfrac{\partial f}{\partial t}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ode-solutions-to">
        <title>Testing Solutions to ODEs</title>

        <definition xml:id="def-solution-to-de">
            <title>Solution to a Differential Equation</title>

            <idx><h>differential equation (solution to)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>solution to a differential equation</term> is a function  which satisfies the rules imposed by the equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The family of functions <m>y(x) = x^2 + C</m> are solutions to the differential equation
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x
                    </md>
                    because <m>y'(x) = \dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2x</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="exponential-growth-intro">
            <title>Exponential Growth</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    The family <m>x(t) = Ce^{kt}</m> is a solution to the differential equation
                    <md>
                        x'(t) = ax
                    </md>
                    for some constant <m>a\in \R</m> because <m>x'(t) = Cke^{kt}</m> and <m>x''(t) = Ck^2e^{kt}</m>. Setting <m>a = k^2</m>, we have <m>x''(t) = aCe^{kt} = ax(t)</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This differential equation models <em>exponential growth</em>. In reality, few things grow exponentially due to constraints in the environment. Despite its lack of realism, we can use the exponential  growth equation to help build other, more useful models.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The acceleration of an object in free-fall is given by <m>a(t) = \dfrac{d^2s}{dt^2} = -g</m> where <m>g</m> is the gravitational constant. Find its position function, knowing that <m>s(0) = 1000</m>m and <m>v(0) = -25</m>m/s.
                </p>
                <p>
                    You likely would have encountered this kind of problem when learning integration/antiderivatives; the solution requires integrating acceleration to find velocity and then using velocity to find position. First, we have
                    <md>
                        v(t) = \dfrac{ds}{dt} = -gt + C
                    </md>
                    Since <m>v(0) = -25</m>, we have <m>C = -25</m> and <m>v(t) = -gt - 25</m>. Next, we integrate again to find position:
                    <md>
                        s(t) = \dfrac{-gt^2}{2} - 25t + C
                    </md>
                    Since <m>s(0) = 1000</m>, we have <m>C = 1000</m> and <m>s(t) = \dfrac{-gt^2}{2} - 25t + 1000</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that the family
                    <m>x(t) = C\cosh(kt) = \dfrac{1}{2}\lrpar{e^t+e^{-t}}</m> is
                    is a solution to the differential equation
                    <md>
                        x''(t) = k^2x
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the differential equation
                    <md>
                        y'' -6y' + 9y = 0
                    </md>
                    Show that the families <m>y_1(x) = C_1e^{3t}</m> and <m>y_2(x) = C_2xe^{3x}</m> are both solutions to the ODE. Is <m>y_3(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)</m> a solution as well?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f_1(x) = c_1x</m>, <m>f_2(x) = c_2x^2</m>, and <m>f_3(x) = c_3x^{-1}</m>. Show that each of these functions is a solution to the differential equation
                    <md>
                        x^3f''' + x^2f'' - 2xf' + 2f = 0
                    </md>
                    What about <m>f_4(x) = f_1(x) + f_3(x)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-linear-combinations-odes-solution">
            <title>Principle of Superposition</title>
            <idx><h>principle of superposition</h></idx>            
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Linear combinations of solutions to a differential equation are also solutions to the same differential equation. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ode-classifying">
        <title>Classifying Differential Equations</title>
            
        <definition xml:id="def-linear-nonlinear-odes">
            <title>Linear/Nonlinear Differential Equations</title>
            <idx><h>differential equations (linear)</h></idx>
            <idx><h>differential equations (nonlinear)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A differential equation is <term>linear</term> if it can be written in the form
                    <md>
                        a_n(x)y^{(n)} + a_{n-1}(x)y^{(n-1)} + \cdots + a_1(x)y' + a_0(x)y = g(x)
                    </md>
                    where the coefficients <m>a_i(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> depend only on the independent variable <m>x</m>. Otherwise, the differential equation is <term>nonlinear</term>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-order-ode">
            <title>Order of a Differential Equation</title>
            <idx><h>differential equation (order)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>order</term> of a differential equation is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <definition xml:id="def-homogeneous-nonhomogeneous-ode">
            <title>(Non)Homogeneous Differential Equation</title>
            <idx><h>differential equation (homogeneous)</h></idx>
            <idx><h>differential equation (nonhomogeneous)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A differential equation is <term>homogeneous</term> if all terms depend on the dependent variable, i.e. if no term is a function of the independent variable alone. Otherwise the equation is <term>nonhomogeneous</term>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <note>
            <p>
                A homogeneous, linear ODE can always be put into the form
                <md>
                    a_n(x)\dfrac{d^ny}{dx^n} + a_{n-1}(x)\dfrac{d^{n-1}y}{dx^{n-1}} + \cdots + a_1(x)\dfrac{dy}{dx} + a_0(x)y = 0
                </md>
            </p>
        </note>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The equation
                    <md>
                        y''' - x^2y' + y = 0
                    </md>
                    has order 3, is linear, and homogeneous. However,
                    <md>
                        y''' - x^2y' + y = x
                    </md>
                    is nonhomogeneous.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the order, linearity status, and homogeneity status of the following ODEs:
                    <ol marker="a.">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\sin(t)\dfrac{d^2x}{dt^2} + \cos(t) x = t^2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>y'' + 3y + 5x = 0</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x'' + tx^2 = t</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </subsection>
</section>